// =============================================================================
// historical-research-course — "Historical Research, Level 1: The 1619
// Project as the Case" — REBUILT from fetched records (DR-0597)
// =============================================================================
// Darrell, 2026-09-24, on the first build of this course: "Where did the rigor
// for this lesson come from?! Not our process!" — "See if the people in the
// 1619 project are accurate based on their process and do not see facts" —
// "Why are we not discussing the experiences of the witnesses?!" — "The reason
// why cities are the way they are or bridges and highways are the way they
// are!" — "Both and training Word first WITH RESEARCH! YOU DIDN'T DO THAT!" —
// "Watering down lessons and issues we need to comprehend!" — "We want the
// history of the American culture to be able to give our children sight to
// see Yahweh's perspectives."
//
// The first build's dates and quotations came from training memory. This
// build carries NOTHING that was not fetched on a GitHub runner first. Every
// claim the project's people made is quoted from the page where they made it
// (the Wayback Machine's dated copies, because the live newspaper pages refuse
// a runner). Every verdict on their process rests on the testimony of people
// inside that process, in their own words (the fact-checker, the scholar not
// consulted, the five historians, the editor). Every experience of the
// enslaved is quoted from the record the witness wrote (Equiano, Jacobs,
// Douglass, Wells). Every fact about the built world comes from the
// government's own records (the Federal Reserve on the FHA, the Supreme Court
// on a covenant, the Federal Highway Administration on the act of 1956, the
// Department of Transportation on the harm it now funds repair of) and one
// witness of the time (Baldwin, 1963). Every dated timeline entry carries a
// source the witness workflow probes on every push (historyTimelineFaults).
// Where a record could not be fetched (John Rolfe's letter; the earlier
// landings of 1526 and 1565), the course says so and does not quote it.
//
// WORD FIRST (DR-0127 / DR-0282). Every competency is a command before it is
// a skill, and every verse is pulled verbatim from the repo's KJV
// (app/public/bible/kjv) and re-pinned by historical-research-course.test.js.
//
// THE ORDER — by sense, not only by date (Darrell, 2026-09-24: "Make sure the
// lessons make sense in their respective locations... they were cultivated in
// chronological order however some lessons may fit better with others").
// Three movements:
//   The standard and the record
//     1. The standard before the claim — the project's own words, sorted into
//        fact, frame and judgment (1 Thessalonians 5:21; Acts 17:11)
//     2. Go to the record — the nearest fetched witness to the landing of
//        1619, stated at exactly the strength the record gives (Luke 1:1-4)
//     3. The witnesses — what the enslaved said, in their own words
//        (Genesis 4:10; Psalms 9:12; Exodus 21:16; Acts 17:26)
//   The project's people, judged on their process and their facts
//     4. Two or three witnesses — the process, in the words of the people who
//        were in it (Deuteronomy 19:15; Proverbs 15:22; Proverbs 18:17)
//     5. A fact and an interpretation — the Revolution claim tested against
//        the record (Proverbs 18:13; John 7:24; Exodus 23:1)
//     6. The correction and the quiet edit — what the magazine did with its
//        own words (Proverbs 28:13; Proverbs 13:18; Proverbs 12:19)
//   The built world and the children
//     7. The city they built — where the project is strongest, proven from
//        the government's own records (Habakkuk 2:12; Isaiah 10:1-2;
//        Isaiah 58:12)
//     8. Write it in order for our children — the sourced paragraph a child
//        can carry, and the sight the Word gives (Habakkuk 2:2;
//        Deuteronomy 6:6-7; Psalms 78:4; Acts 17:26)
// Lesson 2 sits before lesson 3 because the record of the event (the ship)
// precedes the record of the experience (the lives); lesson 7 sits after the
// three process lessons so the project's strength is proven right after its
// faults are, from records outside it; lesson 8 is last because it is the
// deliverable.
//
// TEACH THE WORD, DO NOT DEBATE IT (DR-0098) and SPEAK ESTABLISHED FACT
// (DR-0100). The moral verdict on slavery is settled by the Word and is never
// staged as open. Documented fact is stated as fact, with its date and its
// record: the closed process (the witnesses inside it say so), the three
// supports that fell (the record says so), the open correction and the quiet
// edit (the pages say so), the red lines, the covenant, the highway (the
// government says so). The one genuinely narrow question — how far the
// protection of slavery moved particular colonists — is stated narrowly, as
// the magazine's own corrected sentence states it.
//
// FOUR READING BANDS FROM THIS COMMIT (child ≤ 5.0 FK, youth, teen ≤ 6.0,
// senior ≤ 10.0; every band carries the whole lesson, none is a near copy),
// because "watering down" is the failure this rebuild answers: a child gets
// the whole finding in words a child can read, not a shorter finding.
//
// TEACHING, NOT A SUBSTITUTE FOR THE RECORD — carried on the meta. Every
// document this course names can be read in full at the record it points to,
// and the student is meant to read it.
// =============================================================================

